@@ -39,8 +39,8 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | `GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK` | **OFF** when unset. Only an exact `1` enables the slot; `0`, unset, and every other value leave it disabled, so the Q1_0/Q2_0 arms of `ggml_repack_get_optimal_repack_type()` return `nullptr` and both types fall back to per-row `vec_dot`. **FLIPPED 2026-07-24** from ON-when-unset (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp`, `ggml_arifi_vnni_repack_enabled()`); the new predicate is the exact mirror of the old one. This **deliberately diverges from PrismML's upstream default, which is ON** — the measured reason is in the recommendation cell, and documenting exactly this kind of divergence is what this registry exists for. **Correction 2026-07-24:** the previous wording — that `=0` “restores the exact pre-slot behaviour” — was **false on x86**. Commit `c7645a0df` also replaced the x86 `ggml_vec_dot_q2_0_q8_0`, which sits **outside this toggle** (compile-time `__AVX512VNNI__`/`__AVXVNNI__` selection). `=0` therefore selected a *different* new kernel, not the pre-slot one. | Scope: the CPU repack *type-selection* for `GGML_TYPE_Q1_0`, `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0` and `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128` only, inside `ggml_repack_get_optimal_repack_type()` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp`). ON lets those two types claim the `CPU_REPACK` buffer type and use the interleaved 4×8 GEMV/GEMM kernels (AVX512-VNNI on x86, NEON+DP on Arm); OFF falls back to per-row `vec_dot`. It does **not** touch Q4_0/Q4_K/Q8_0 repack, and is inert on any model containing no Q1_0/Q2_0 tensors.<br>**g128 added 2026-07-29 (lane-110F).** `Q2_0_G128` has its own dispatch arm, its own kernel instantiations and its own block type; it does NOT share the g64 kernels, because a g128 row in a g64 kernel is mis-strided silent wrong math rather than a crash. CORRECTNESS measured (token-identical to the scalar path, max |delta logprob| 0.021-0.040, inside the F-05 band; repack buffer 1759.50 MiB = 252 of 254 ternary tensors on Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128). SPEED MEASURED 2026-07-29 on g128 (RIG-A, Ternary-Bonsai-8B-Q2_0.g128, -ngl 0 --no-host -c 2048 -t 8, modes INTERLEAVED across 3 replicates x 5 rolls, n=12 per cell, all ranges disjoint): decode 2.01 -> 6.67 tok/s (+231.6%) at mode 1 and 6.69 (+232.6%) at mode 2; prompt 59.09 -> 17.44 (-70.5%) at mode 1 but 85.84 (+45.3%) at mode 2. The g128 scalar path was 2.01 tok/s, so this is a 3.3x decode win, much larger than g64's +22-24% because g64 already had a fast scalar vec_dot and g128 never did. NOTE: mode 2 wins BOTH axes on g128, unlike g64 where the prompt gain overlapped and was reported as noise. **The mechanism for the prompt win is no longer ASSUMED — placements were counted 2026-07-30 (lane-110G, `GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2`, untimed).** Mode 2's per-graph MUL_MAT placement is IDENTICAL to mode 0's across all 24 graph dumps, so dual residency provably moves no work. But the CPU share of the offloaded graph is **4 of 253 (1.6%)**, SMALLER than the 0.5B g64 models' 4 of 169 (2.4%) — so F-06's former rationale ("on an 8B that CPU share is large enough to matter") was false. The four CPU-resident MUL_MATs are `ffn_gate-35`, `ffn_up-35`, `ffn_out-35` and `result_output`: the final layer's whole FFN plus the output head, three of them among the largest matmuls in the model. A 1.6% share by COUNT is not a 1.6% share by COST. Per-op timing is still owed before the whole +45.3% is attributed to them. **GPU-vs-CPU on ONE binary, same session (lane-110G):** GPU `-ngl 99` 327.38 prompt / 34.40 decode vs mode 2's 85.62 / 6.56 — the GPU tier is 3.8x on prompt and 5.2x on decode, and 18.3x the mode-0 CPU floor on decode. The former "roughly 4x on both axes" was a CROSS-BINARY citation and is withdrawn. The +327%/+442% prompt and +20%/+24% decode figures elsewhere in this row remain g64 numbers and must not be read as g128 ones. The x86 Q2_0 `vec_dot` fast path added alongside it is **not** covered by this toggle — see the default cell. | Reachable for a **g64** Q1_0/Q2_0 model and, since 2026-07-29 (lane-110F), for a **g128** `Q2_0_G128` model, and on a Vulkan-enabled build only with `--no-host`. **ADJUDICATED 2026-07-24 (bit-divergence round).** Bit-identity is **not achievable and was never the right bar** — proven against upstream's own repack types on this same binary (cost cell). The slot is now **correct and measured**. **DEFAULT OFF is IMPLEMENTED, not merely recommended** (flipped 2026-07-24, verified on a rebuilt binary — see the cost cell). The reason is unchanged: on a Vulkan-enabled build repacked weights lose GPU-offload eligibility and prompt processing regresses ~77–88%; on a CPU-only path the same slot is a **+327% to +442% prompt and +20% to +24% decode** win, so it stays compiled in and one `GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=1` away. Set it to `1` for a CPU-only deployment; leave it unset everywhere else. The lane merges, not the executor. | **NaN FIXED (2026-07-24). Bit-divergence ADJUDICATED as INHERENT to the repack design, not a residual bug. Performance MEASURED (2026-07-24).**<br><br>**Root cause (one defect class, four sites).** PrismML's tree has `QK2_0 = 128`, so `QK2_0/QK8_0 = 4` and their kernels hardcode the activation-block stride as the literal `4`. This fork is canonical g64 (`QK2_0 = 64`, `ggml/src/ggml-common.h:187`), where the ratio is **2**. Every ported site that hardcoded `4` advanced the `block_q8_0` activation pointer twice as fast as its loop consumed, running past the packed activation buffer. **This is a downstream g64 integration defect, not a defect in PrismML's tree** — at g128 every one of these literals is correct.<br><br>**Fix A — the GEMM (4 lines, 2 files).** `ggml_gemm_q2_0_4x8_q8_0` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/repack.cpp:6664,6681`) and `ggml_gemm_q2_0_4x8_q8_0_generic` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp:2767,2786`): `(4 * y * nb)` → `(y * nb * (QK2_0 / QK8_0))` and `a_ptr + 4 * l + k` → `a_ptr + l * (QK2_0 / QK8_0) + k`, matching the form `ggml_gemv_q2_0_4x8_q8_0` already used correctly at `:6614`. Out-of-bounds `a_blk->d[m]` had been decoding as FP16 NaN and poisoning the FMA accumulator. The **Q1_0 arms were deliberately left alone**: `QK1_0 = 128`, so their hardcoded `4` is correct for this fork's current Q1 format — a portability hazard, not a present defect.<br><br>**Fix B — the control arm was itself broken (2 lines, separate commit).** The ported x86 `ggml_vec_dot_q2_0_q8_0` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c`, added by `c7645a0df`) carried the same g128 literals in **both** its VNNI branch and its scalar `#else`: `for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++)` over `&y[i * 4 + k]`, plus `&x[i].qs[k * 8]` reading at offsets 16 and 24 of a **16-byte** `block_q2_0.qs`. Since it is compile-time-selected, `GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=0` could not disable it. **Consequence: every ON/OFF comparison recorded in this slot before 2026-07-24 compared one broken kernel against a differently broken kernel, and none of them adjudicated anything.** The generic `ggml_vec_dot_q2_0_q8_0_generic` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/quants.c:203-204`) was already correct (`k < 2`, `y[i * 2 + k]`); the x86 arm now matches it via `QK2_0 / QK8_0`. This also retires the prior registry claim that this fast path was “bit-exact with the scalar loop it replaces” — **falsified**.<br><br>**Engagement proven, both arms, both models** (llama-server per F-085, `-ngl 0 -c 1024 -t 8 --no-host`, temp 0, top_k 1, seed 42, `cache_prompt` false, fresh server per arm, env flip only, same binary). ON: `load_tensors: CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 4.68 MiB` (Q8_0 base + 4× `ffn_down`) and `95.98 MiB` (all-Q2_0). OFF: **no `CPU_REPACK` line at all**. **Correction:** the prior row claimed `CPU_Mapped` “grows by the same amount” when repack is off. It does **not** — `CPU_Mapped` was `625.75 MiB` in *both* arms; the repack buffer is an additional allocation and the mmap accounting is unchanged. (The prior run's own numbers, 322.28 + 95.98 ON vs 323.45 OFF, already contradicted it.)<br><br>**Result, `qwen2.5-0.5b-light-q8_0-4xffndownQ2_0-g64.gguf`, 11-token prefill (GEMM path):** the NaN is **gone**. ON is finite, coherent and deterministic 3/3 (`md5 bf102b7715220e6f6df074626ede9a0e`), and after Fix B the OFF arm produces the **token-identical** continuation — same md5, all 32 tokens, deterministic 3/3 in both arms. Before Fix B the arms disagreed completely (ON coherent, OFF `“ering!!!!…”`). Independent check: the unquantized `qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-fp16.gguf` on the identical prompt continues `“ the dog jumps over the lazy cat. What is the next…”` (6/6 identical), which the repacked arm tracks closely.<br><br>**Bit-identity: NOT achieved, and ADJUDICATED as INHERENT (2026-07-24).** The ON/OFF logprob divergence is real (both arms internally deterministic, 3/3 rolls each) but it is **a property of llama.cpp's repack design, not of this port**. Proven with an upstream-blessed control: four additional models were quantised from the *same* `qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-fp16.gguf` source with the *same* Q8_0 base and the *same* four tensors (`blk.0-3.ffn_down`, `4864x896`) carrying an **upstream** repack type instead of Q2_0, then run through the identical protocol on the identical binary. `--no-repack` was used as a uniform OFF for every arm and is byte-for-byte equivalent to `GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK=0` on the Q2_0 model (identical logprob fingerprint `3dea625c`, 3/3 rolls). Results, max absolute delta-logprob over 32 tokens: **ours Q2_0 4x8 = `0.061002`** (mean `0.0123761`, token-identical); **upstream Q2_K 8x8 = `0.140226`** (mean `0.0263535`, token-identical); **upstream Q4_K 8x8 = `1.39052`** (mean `0.328262`, and it **changes the generated text**). Upstream's own blessed repack types are therefore *less* faithful to their scalar paths than this port is to its own. Mechanism class is acknowledged upstream in-tree: `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/repack.cpp:210` comments that the repack activation quantiser is divided by `127.f` "to mirror results in quantize_row_q8_0" — a *mirror*, not an identity. **The prior demand for bit-identity was the wrong acceptance bar and is withdrawn.**<br><br>**Upstream 8x8 repack SEGFAULTS on this box — pre-existing, NOT this branch.** Three of the five upstream repack types could not be run at all: Q4_0 (`q4_0_8x8`), IQ4_NL (`iq4_nl_8x8`) and MXFP4 (`mxfp4_8x8`) all abort with `0xC0000005` ACCESS_VIOLATION once `--no-host` makes `CPU_REPACK` reachable, after the buffer is allocated (`CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 9.35 / 9.35 / 8.83 MiB`). gdb frame 0 is `ggml_gemv_q4_0_8x8_q8_0`, frame 1 `ggml::cpu::repack::tensor_traits<block_q4_0, 8ll, 8ll, (ggml_type)8>::compute_forward`, reached from `llama_decode` on a libgomp worker. **Attribution settled: stock upstream `llama-upstream-b10068` (`src/llama-upstream-b10068/build-vulkan/bin/llama-server.exe`) reproduces the identical crash on the identical model** — and this branch is strictly additive over its own base commit `f7c4d9d2c` (`git diff --numstat f7c4d9d2c..e32bf4149`: zero deletions in `repack.cpp`, `arch/x86/repack.cpp` and `arch/x86/quants.c`; the only deletion across the branch's nine commits is one `arch-fallback.h` alias). **Topology correction — RESOLVED 2026-07-24 (rebase round).** `f7c4d9d2c` is *not* master; it is the tag `pre-linearize-2026-07-24`, a merge commit on a parallel lineage. Master was then LINEARIZED to **`279f59f11`** (linear series, no fork merge commits); `67c83a6ec` is now an intermediate commit on that line, not the tip. The two lines diverged at `e70805586`. **The rebase is DONE:** `git rebase --onto master pre-linearize-2026-07-24 topic/vnni-repack` replayed all twelve commits with **zero conflicts**, and the resulting delta is byte-identical to the pre-rebase delta (same `git diff` blob hash `1330ce15`), so no content was lost or altered. The three dropped merge commits (`f7c4d9d2c`, `42c1b19be`, `e2ac92c40`) are gone from the branch ancestry and master is now a strict ancestor of it. **No conflict with `a54f6cae1` (Q2_0_G128 dispatch) arose, and none was possible:** that commit touches only `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp` (seven switch-case labels: add, add1, acc, out_prod, set, get_rows, clamp), a file this branch never modifies. No master-only commit touches any of the seven files this branch changes. The two mechanisms are disjoint in source and kept disjoint at runtime by the `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128` early return at `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp:5164`. **Post-rebase sanity, re-measured on the rebased binary** (llama-server, `-ngl 0 --no-host -c 1024 -t 8 -v`, temp 0, top_k 1, seed 42, `cache_prompt` false): the NaN remains gone — `qwen2.5-0.5b-light-q8_0-4xffndownQ2_0-g64.gguf` ON returns coherent text with `CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 4.68 MiB`, OFF returns coherent text with **no** `CPU_REPACK` line at all, and `CPU_Mapped` is `625.75 MiB` in *both* arms (re-confirming the mmap-accounting correction below). The g128 path also still works: the native type-43 `Ternary-Bonsai-27B-Abliterated-LowDeg-Q2_0.g128.gguf` loads (`CPU_Mapped = 6822.71 MiB`, tensors reported as `q2_0_g128`), answers “The capital city of France is” with “ Paris. Paris is the capital city of France.”, aborts nowhere, and logs `token_embd.weight (q2_0_g128) (and 850 others) cannot be used with preferred buffer type CPU_REPACK, using CPU instead` with **no** `CPU_REPACK` buffer — the tripwire holds with the slot ON. **Gate default was ON when unset at the time of the measurements above** (`repack.cpp:5144-5150`), which did *not* match this row's own merge recommendation. **It has since been FLIPPED to OFF (2026-07-24)** and re-verified on a freshly built server binary (`recipe diff` PASS 309/309 first, then a `llama-server`-target build, exit 0), same protocol as above on `qwen2.5-0.5b-light-q8_0-4xffndownQ2_0-g64.gguf`, `-ngl 0 --no-host -c 1024 -t 8 -v`, fresh server per arm, reaped between arms, env flip only: **unset** produces **no** `load_tensors: CPU_REPACK model buffer size` line at all, **`=1`** produces `CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 4.68 MiB`. The loader's per-tensor fallback line appears in both arms and counts **290** ineligible tensors when unset against **286** with `=1` — the difference is exactly the four `blk.0-3.ffn_down` Q2_0 tensors the slot claims. Both arms return coherent text, no NaN, and `CPU_Mapped` is `625.75 MiB` in both (re-confirming the mmap-accounting correction above). Performance was **not** re-measured and did not need to be: the flip changes which branch an unset environment takes, not either kernel. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/vnni-default-flip/`. `--no-warmup` does not avoid the repack when the slot is on. The 4-wide/8-inter kernels — ours (`q2_0_4x8`, NB_COLS=4) and upstream's Q8_K pair (`q2_K_8x8`, `q4_K_8x8`) — run fine. Not this slot's defect; recorded for the lane.<br><br>**Performance: MEASURED 2026-07-24, from scratch.** All prior figures from this slot remain **void** (the `+58%` decode and `+354%` prompt gaps came from a NaN-poisoned ON arm; every pre-`2026-07-24` gap used the Fix-B baseline that read out of bounds). New protocol: llama-server per F-085, two models (`qwen2.5-0.5b-mixed-q8_0-ffnQ2_0-g64.gguf`, 72 Q2_0 tensors, `CPU_REPACK = 84.16 MiB`; and `qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-Q2_0-g64.gguf`, all-Q2_0, `CPU_REPACK = 95.98 MiB`; OFF arms show **no** `CPU_REPACK` line), `-ngl 0 --no-host -c 2048 -t 8`, 419-token prefill, `n_predict 128`, temp 0, top_k 1, seed 42, `cache_prompt` false, **three interleaved ON/OFF replicate pairs x 5 rolls**, fresh server per arm, reaped and verified gone between arms, free RAM `7.40–7.69 GB` at every launch. Timings read from `slot print_timing`; roll 1 of each arm dropped as a cold first request, leaving **n=9 per arm — every value listed, no means substituted**.<br>**CPU-only (`-dev none`, the true kernel-vs-kernel comparison).** *mixed* prompt tok/s — ON `612.81/619.65/629.06/638.73/643.72/647.85/648.09/655.15/670.67` vs OFF `142.15/143.14/143.35/143.81/150.85/152.44/152.65/152.90/153.16` (**+326.7%** median, ranges DISJOINT); *mixed* decode tok/s — ON `49.23/49.49/49.64/49.67/49.71/49.72/49.75/49.77/49.80` vs OFF `39.46/40.16/40.21/40.31/41.46/41.60/41.67/41.69/41.76` (**+19.9%**, DISJOINT). *all-Q2_0* prompt tok/s — ON `602.65/639.27/640.38/657.49/738.80/740.08/761.58/769.50/772.82` vs OFF `128.48/129.33/129.72/135.24/136.35/136.73/136.98/136.98/137.44` (**+441.8%**, DISJOINT); *all-Q2_0* decode tok/s — ON `51.24/51.27/51.31/51.39/51.39/51.54/51.56/51.58/51.60` vs OFF `38.71/39.28/39.71/40.14/41.59/41.62/41.65/41.75/41.79` (**+23.6%**, DISJOINT).<br>**Noise check passes explicitly**: largest within-arm spread is `3.08` tok/s (decode) and `170.17` tok/s (all-Q2_0 prompt ON) against between-arm gaps of `9.80` and `602.45`; ON and OFF ranges do not overlap in any of the eight comparisons. This is the opposite of the earlier `+31%` slot that was noise.<br>**Vulkan-enabled (default build, `-dev` unset) — the merge-relevant caveat.** Decode still wins: *mixed* ON `49.32/49.33/49.34/49.37/49.49/49.99/50.00/50.21/50.21` vs OFF `39.70/39.98/40.23/40.84/41.20/41.28/41.62/41.68/41.74` (**+20.1%**); *all-Q2_0* ON `51.11/51.20/51.38/51.46/51.66/51.76/51.76/51.78/51.96` vs OFF `39.99/40.06/40.10/40.13/40.25/40.31/40.31/41.45/41.61` (**+28.4%**). But **prompt processing collapses**: *mixed* ON `213.82/216.32/216.77/219.28/220.69/221.12/221.83/241.40/258.81` vs OFF `952.81/956.58/959.42/959.92/961.95/966.47/967.16/969.51/971.34` (**-77.1%**); *all-Q2_0* ON `90.33/94.46/99.75/114.08/114.27/116.23/116.24/117.51/118.59` vs OFF `927.01/944.32/950.46/951.14/957.05/958.06/961.67/965.85/972.70` (**-88.1%**). **Cause, evidenced not assumed:** weights in the `CPU_REPACK` buffer are no longer eligible for large-batch GPU offload. All-Q2_0 ON logs `Vulkan0 compute buffer size = 5.26 MiB / Vulkan_Host = 34.01 MiB, graph splits = 286`, while OFF logs `Vulkan0 = 37.72 MiB / Vulkan_Host = 5.51 MiB, graph splits = 335` — the batch matmuls that ran on the iGPU move back to the CPU. Decode (`bs=1`, `graph splits = 1`) is CPU-side in both arms, which is why the decode win survives. **Therefore: default OFF on a Vulkan build; ON is the right default only for a CPU-only path.**<br><br>**Not attempted, recorded:** wiring these kernels to `GGML_TYPE_Q2_0_G128`. The base block layouts are byte-identical, but this fork has **no** `block_q2_0_g128x4` type and no g128 repack path, and the existing g64 `block_q2_0x4` is 72 B — dispatching type-43 into it would be silent wrong math. A real g128 path needs its own x4 struct (`d[4] + qs[128]` = 136 B), g128 repack/make functions, block-size-parameterised kernels, separate dispatch and scalar-equivalence testing. Future slot.<br><br>**HTTP 500 reproduction, corrected.** The server guard "The model produced output that does not match the expected Content-only format" was previously recorded as an ON-arm, all-Q2_0 phenomenon. It is **not repack-related**: on a long repetitive prompt it fires in **all 12 performance arms — ON and OFF, both models** (`CPU_REPACK = 84.16/95.98 MiB` and `0.0 MiB` alike), while the short prompt passes the guard in every arm. It is a response-format guard on degenerate output; generation completes and the server stays alive, so timings remain readable from `slot print_timing`.<br><br>**Still unexercised:** GEMV bit-exactness against `vec_dot` in isolation (`-ub 1` still the only clean lever and it remains untested here); the generic `repack.cpp` GEMM fix, which is unreachable on this box because the AVX512 branch returns first; and every Arm/NEON arm. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/vnni-g64-fix/` (NaN fix) and `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/vnni-adjudication/` (adjudication + performance). |
